<!DOCTYPE article PUBLIC "-//NLM//DTD Journal Archiving and Interchange DTD v2.3 20070202//EN" "archivearticle.dtd"><article xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" article-type="research-article"><?properties open_access?><front><journal-meta><journal-id journal-id-type="nlm-ta">J Exp Med</journal-id><journal-title>The Journal of Experimental Medicine</journal-title><issn pub-type="ppub">0022-1007</issn><issn pub-type="epub">1540-9538</issn><publisher><publisher-name>The Rockefeller University Press</publisher-name></publisher></journal-meta><article-meta><article-id pub-id-type="pmid">5112206</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="pmc">2139015</article-id><article-categories><subj-group subj-group-type="heading"><subject>Article</subject></subj-group></article-categories><title-group><article-title><italic>H-2</italic>-LINKED GENETIC CONTROL OF IMMUNE RESPONSIVENESS TO OVALBUMIN AND OVOMUCOID</article-title></title-group><contrib-group><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Vaz</surname><given-names>Nelson M.</given-names></name></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Phillips-Quagliata</surname><given-names>Julia M.</given-names></name></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Levine</surname><given-names>Bernard B.</given-names></name></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Vaz</surname><given-names>Eda M.</given-names></name></contrib></contrib-group><aff id="N0x1dd9a60N0x3ccdc80">From the Departments of Medicine and Pathology, New York University School of Medicine, New York 10016</aff><pub-date pub-type="ppub"><day>31</day><month>10</month><year>1971</year></pub-date><volume>134</volume><issue>5</issue><fpage>1335</fpage><lpage>1348</lpage><history><date date-type="received"><day>7</day><month>7</month><year>1971</year></date></history><permissions><copyright-statement>Copyright &#x000a9; 1971 by The Rockefeller University Press</copyright-statement></permissions><abstract><p> Immune responsiveness of inbred mice to low doses of ovalbumin or ovomucoid is under control of single dominant genes closely linked to alleles of the <italic>H-2</italic> locus. High responsiveness to ovomucoid is linked with the <italic>H-2<sup>a</sup></italic> and <italic>H-2<sup>k</sup></italic> alleles, and to ovalbumin with the <italic>H-2<sup>b</sup>, H-2<sup>d</sup></italic>, and <italic>H-2<sup>q</sup></italic> alleles. </p></abstract></article-meta></front></article>